{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3244","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3244","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3244\",\"parent_revision\":\"d1dbeb3d19c631cc468c71e2931b1d6da2a6e02c\",\"root_revision\":\"9eb1f6bcbedece679af97aa170e63afaf253675f05e2d89f28aa863cb7deb0d1\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-27\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nprocess-siren (plugin-creator:process-siren agent / process-siren:woo-sailor skill) produces Mermaid diagrams inline in the target file under a fidelity mandate \u2014 one node per prose step/condition/outcome, nothing merged or omitted. When the target is a skill's SKILL.md, a large diagram can push the file's token count over skilllint's SK006/AS005 threshold (4400 tokens), triggering a warning that must then be resolved by hand.\\n\\nObserved: running woo-sailor's full-step-fidelity rebuild against plugins/development-harness/skills/multi-perspective-review/SKILL.md's Dispatch Flow diagram (PR #3243) pushed the file from 4306 to 4977 tokens, over the threshold. A separate follow-up agent then had to manually relocate the diagram to a references/dispatch-flow.md file behind a context pointer to clear the warning \u2014 this repo's existing progressive-disclosure convention (mattpocock-skills:writing-for-agents) already supports this pattern, and references/acceptance-test-guide.md in the same skill is a working precedent.\\n\\nprocess-siren should make this decision itself instead of requiring a manual follow-up every time: when a diagram it produces is reference/verification material that some execution paths don't need (information-hierarchy tier 3), and inlining it would push the target file over the token-budget threshold, it should extract the diagram to a disclosed reference file reached by a context pointer, rather than always inlining. When a diagram is load-bearing content every execution path needs (information-hierarchy tier 1), it should stay inline regardless of size \u2014 a blanket always-extract-when-large rule would incorrectly push genuinely load-bearing diagrams behind a pointer that every invocation needs to see immediately.\\n\\n**User-provided context**: Tie the extraction threshold to skilllint's own 4400-token SK006/AS005 line (or a diagram-specific line count). Update the plugin-creator:process-siren agent definition and/or the process-siren:woo-sailor skill's dispatch logic to make this decision automatically. The extracted file should follow the existing references/*.md + context-pointer pattern already used elsewhere in this repo, not a bare .mmd/.mermaid file.\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-27T01:36:09.699754Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Snapshot: process-siren should auto-disclose large Mermaid diagrams to references/ instead of always inlining\\nDate: 2026-08-27\\nGoal: Change process-siren/woo-sailor's diagram-authoring behavior so it never leaves duplicated diagram+prose sequencing in a target file, and discloses genuinely reference-only diagrams to references/ when inlining would exceed the file's token budget.\\nConditions:\\n1. What agent file(s) currently define process-siren's diagram-authoring behavior, and does that definition already contain any prose-duplication-avoidance or placement logic? | Status: DERIVABLE (read plugin-creator:process-siren agent definition and process-siren:woo-sailor skill directly \u2014 not yet read this session, only their tool-listing descriptions are known)\\n2. Is skilllint's 4400-token SK006/AS005 threshold queryable/importable by process-siren, or is it a constant that would need to be duplicated/hardcoded into the agent's own logic? | Status: DERIVABLE (grep skilllint source/config for the threshold definition and any exposed API)\\n3. Does this repo have any existing prose/diagram-duplication detection tooling (skilllint checks, markdownlint rules, or similar) that could be reused rather than building new detection logic? | Status: DERIVABLE (search skilllint and markdownlint-cli2 rule sets)\\n4. Is the \\\"load-bearing for every execution path\\\" vs \\\"reference/verification material\\\" distinction mechanically decidable by an LLM agent from the target file's structure alone (e.g., \\\"referenced from more than one Step\\\" vs \\\"referenced from a single cross-check section\\\"), or does it require judgment that resists a hard rule? | Status: AVAILABLE (established this session \u2014 a diagram every execution path needs stays inline; the working example is dh:work-backlog-item's routing sections, which are consulted on every invocation of that route)\\n5. Should retrofit detection (finding and resolving duplication already present in files process-siren did not just edit) be in scope for this item, versus scoping process-siren to only avoid introducing new duplication going forward? | Status: AVAILABLE (Anti-Goal 1 already scopes retrofit out \u2014 the one identified concrete retrofit instance is tracked separately as #3246)\\nAVAILABLE count: 2\\nDERIVABLE count: 3\\nMISSING count: 0\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 surfaced while running the multi-perspective-review skill-tightening pipeline (PRs #3240-#3243)\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-27\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Design Note\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-27T00:58:02.950912Z</sub>\\n\\nprocess-siren's purpose is a process clarifier and communicator for the reading agent, not a diagram-drawing agent \u2014 it already does the harder work first (analyzes the whole picture before drawing, follows workflow/graph design methodology when it does draw, then validates Mermaid syntax and layout). That framing should drive the inline-vs-disclose decision this item asks for: the trigger is not simply \\\"the diagram is large\\\" or \\\"the file crossed a token threshold\\\" \u2014 it is whether keeping the diagram inline still serves process clarity for the agent that has to follow it, versus whether disclosure would serve clarity better (or at least not harm it) while relieving token pressure. A load-bearing diagram every execution path needs stays inline even if large, because inlining IS what serves clarity there; a diagram that is reference/verification material only some paths consult is a stronger disclosure candidate on clarity grounds alone, and the token threshold is corroborating evidence, not the primary trigger.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-27T01:03:45.940092Z</sub>\\n\\nSharper correction (supersedes the \\\"inline vs disclose\\\" framing above as the primary lens): the real failure mode this session's passes (#3240, #3241, #3243, #3245 against multi-perspective-review/SKILL.md) never fixed is prose/diagram duplication, not diagram placement. Agents consume Mermaid as notation, not as a rendered picture \u2014 they never \\\"see\\\" the diagram, they parse the text. That means the diagram's value is notation density and branching precision, not visual communication. The anti-pattern is keeping a prose walkthrough of the same sequencing/branching the diagram already encodes \u2014 that is the same \\\"duplication \u2014 same meaning in more than one place\\\" failure mattpocock-skills:writing-for-agents already names, and it inflates the file's token footprint independent of whether the diagram itself is inline or disclosed to references/.\\n\\nThe corrected design: the diagram should be the sole authoritative representation of sequencing and branching logic. Prose should carry only what the notation cannot \u2014 exact MCP call signatures, payload/field shapes, schema pointers \u2014 not a second narration of the same branches. This repo already has a working precedent for this shape: dh:work-backlog-item's routing sections contain only a Mermaid diagram with zero parallel prose re-describing the same branches.\\n\\nConcrete follow-up (separate, more foundational than the disclosure heuristic above): multi-perspective-review/SKILL.md's Steps 1-7 prose still fully restates the same branching the (now-disclosed) Dispatch Flow diagram encodes. Converging these to one representation would likely eliminate most of the token pressure that motivated the disclosure work in the first place \u2014 the auto-disclosure heuristic above becomes a secondary concern once the duplication itself is removed, since a de-duplicated skill is naturally smaller.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"cf31a5f714a389fab28704b543c5f7a6b19f481fd494c088d6484be8491c38ab\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQ9i9LA\"}"}